crates.summary:
Coordinate frames and the transformations between them.

This crate defines positions that carry their reference frame in the
type, so a position expressed in one frame cannot be passed where another
is expected. The two frames are the Earth-centered inertial (ECI) frame
and the Earth-centered Earth-fixed (ECEF) frame.

Three position representations are offered:

- [`Eci`], an inertial Cartesian position in meters
- [`Ecef`], an Earth-fixed Cartesian position in meters
- [`Geodetic`], a latitude, longitude, and height above the WGS 84
  ellipsoid

The transformation between [`Eci`] and [`Ecef`] is the rotation defined
by the Earth Rotation Angle, which `apsis-frames` computes. The
transformation between [`Ecef`] and [`Geodetic`] uses the WGS 84
ellipsoid from `apsis-geodesy`.

```
use apsis_coord::{Ecef, Eci, Geodetic, WGS84};
use apsis_frames::EarthRotationAngle;
use apsis_math::Vec3;

// A station on the equator at zero longitude.
let station = Geodetic::new(0.0, 0.0, 0.0);
let fixed = station.to_ecef();
assert!((fixed.vector().x - WGS84.semi_major_axis()).abs() < 1e-6);

// Rotating it into the inertial frame preserves its length.
let inertial = fixed.to_eci(EarthRotationAngle::from_ut1_days(0.0));
assert!((inertial.vector().norm() - fixed.vector().norm()).abs() < 1e-6);
let _ = Eci::new(Vec3::X);
```

Sources:

- Earth Rotation Angle: *IERS Conventions (2010)*, equation 8.32, as
  implemented by `apsis-frames`.
- Geodetic conversions and the WGS 84 ellipsoid: NIMA TR8350.2, third
  edition, 2000, as implemented by `apsis-geodesy`.

The SGP4 and SDP4 analytical orbit propagator.

The propagator takes the mean orbital elements of a two-line element set
and returns the position and velocity of the satellite at a requested
instant. It is the model that the element sets are fitted to, so it is
the correct way to use them; a general purpose numerical integrator
given the same elements would drift away from the published positions.

Two branches exist and the propagator selects between them by orbital
period. A satellite with a period below 225 minutes is propagated with
SGP4, which models the secular effect of the Earth's oblateness and a
drag term. A satellite at or above that period is propagated with SDP4,
which adds the resonance and the luni-solar perturbations that dominate
at that altitude.

Inputs and outputs:

- Elements are the mean elements from [`apsis_tle::TwoLineElement`].
  Angles in the element set are in degrees and mean motion is in
  revolutions per day; this crate converts to radians and radians per
  minute internally.
- The output is expressed in the True Equator Mean Equinox (TEME) frame,
  with position in kilometers and velocity in kilometers per second. TEME
  is the frame the model defines; converting it to an Earth-fixed frame
  requires the Earth rotation and the equation of the equinoxes and is
  not part of this crate.
- Time is measured from the element set epoch, in minutes.

Source: the algorithm follows Vallado et al., *Revisiting Spacetrack
Report #3*, AIAA 2006-6753, 2006, and the revised implementation
distributed with that paper.

```
use apsis_sgp4::{GravityModel, Satellite};
use apsis_tle::TwoLineElement;

let text = concat!(
    "1 25544U 98067A   08264.51782528 -.00002182  00000-0 -11606-4 0  2927\r\n",
    "2 25544  51.6416 247.4627 0006703 130.5360 325.0288 15.72125391563537\r\n",
);
let element = TwoLineElement::parse(text).expect("sample is well formed");
let satellite = Satellite::from_element(&element, GravityModel::WGS72)
    .expect("sample elements are propagatable");

// Propagate to the epoch of the elements.
let state = satellite.propagate_minutes(0.0).expect("epoch state exists");
// The position of a low Earth satellite is a few thousand kilometers
// from the center of the Earth.
assert!(state.position.norm() > 6_600.0);
assert!(state.position.norm() < 7_200.0);
```

Ephemeris sampling and interpolation.

A propagator gives the state of a satellite at any instant, but calling
it millions of times is wasteful when the result is only needed to draw a
track or to search for a crossing. This crate records the state at a
grid of times, called samples, and answers queries between them by
interpolation.

The two halves are deliberately independent:

- [`Ephemeris`] is a table of times and states. It knows nothing about
  where the states came from, so a table propagated by SGP4, computed by
  two-body theory, or read from a file all work the same way.
- [`Sampler`] fills a table by calling a function that produces a state
  from a time. In this workspace that function is normally
  `apsis_sgp4::Satellite::propagate_at`, but the sampler only requires
  a closure, so a test can use an analytic function.

Interpolation is Hermite cubic, which uses both the position and the
velocity at each end of an interval. Using the velocity is what makes it
accurate: a cubic through two positions alone has no information about
how fast the satellite is moving, while Hermite reconstruction matches
the slope at both ends and so reproduces a smooth orbit far better for
the same sample spacing. On a circular orbit the error is of order the
fourth power of the step, so halving the step cuts the error about
sixteen fold.

Times are in seconds from an origin chosen by the caller, matching the
rest of the orbit layer. Positions are in kilometers and velocities in
kilometers per second.

```
use apsis_ephemeris::{Sample, Sampler};
use apsis_kepler::{GravityParameter, KeplerianElements};

// A circular orbit at the Earth's equatorial radius.
let elements = KeplerianElements {
    semi_major_axis: 7_000.0,
    eccentricity: 0.0,
    inclination: 0.9,
    right_ascension: 0.0,
    argument_of_perigee: 0.0,
    true_anomaly: 0.0,
};
let state_at = |seconds: f64| {
    let mean = 2.0 * core::f64::consts::PI * seconds
        / elements.period(GravityParameter::WGS84);
    let state = elements
        .with_mean_anomaly(mean)
        .to_state(GravityParameter::WGS84)
        .expect("the orbit is valid");
    Sample::new(seconds, state.position, state.velocity)
};

let sampler = Sampler::new(0.0, 600.0, 60.0);
let ephemeris = sampler.sample(state_at).expect("the grid is usable");
assert_eq!(ephemeris.len(), 11);

// A query between two samples is interpolated.
let state = ephemeris.state_at(30.0).expect("the time is covered");
assert!(state.position.norm() > 6_900.0);
```

Orbital state facade that integrates the orbit layer.

This crate is the single entry point to the orbit layer. It re-exports
the element parsing, the analytical propagator, and the two-body model,
and adds one type that ties them together.

[`Orbit`] holds a satellite prepared for propagation together with the
gravity model it was initialised against, and offers the conversions
between the two ways of describing an orbit that the layer supports:

- Mean elements propagated by SGP4, which is the model a two-line
  element set is written for and the only way to reproduce the published
  positions of a catalogued satellite.
- Instantaneous Keplerian elements, which describe the osculating
  two-body orbit at one instant and are useful for inspection and for
  planning a maneuver.

The two descriptions are not interchangeable. Propagating mean elements
with the two-body model, or osculating elements with SGP4, gives a
different and wrong answer; the conversions here are explicit about which
direction they go.

Two further operations are built on the propagation and exposed here
because they need the same gravity parameter to stay consistent:

- [`Orbit::sample_ephemeris`] records the track on a time grid so that it
  can be queried by interpolation instead of by propagation, which is
  what a coverage or access search wants.
- [`Orbit::apply_burn`] applies a finite maneuver to the osculating state
  and returns the resulting elements, which is what a maneuver plan
  produces.

```
use apsis_orbit::{GravityModel, Orbit};
use apsis_tle::TwoLineElement;

let text = concat!(
    "1 25544U 98067A   08264.51782528 -.00002182  00000-0 -11606-4 0  2927\r\n",
    "2 25544  51.6416 247.4627 0006703 130.5360 325.0288 15.72125391563537\r\n",
);
let element = TwoLineElement::parse(text).expect("sample is well formed");
let orbit = Orbit::from_element(&element, GravityModel::WGS72)
    .expect("sample elements are propagatable");

let state = orbit.propagate_minutes(60.0).expect("state exists");
assert!(state.speed() > 7.0);

// The osculating elements at that instant.
let osculating = orbit.osculating_elements(60.0).expect("state exists");
assert!(osculating.eccentricity < 0.01);
```

Ground station access windows.

A satellite is accessible from a ground station during the intervals when
it rises above the station's elevation mask. Each such interval is a
pass, or access window, and the sequence of windows over a period is the
access schedule. This crate finds those windows from a propagated orbit
and reports their start, end, duration, and the geometry at their peak.

The windows are found by sampling the elevation angle at a fixed step and
refining each crossing where the elevation passes the mask. The sampling
step sets the shortest pass that can be found, and the refinement gives
the crossing times to a tolerance independent of the step.

The elevation is evaluated from the satellite position in the
Earth-fixed frame, so the caller supplies the transformation from the
orbit frame. In this workspace that is the conversion in `apsis-coord`
with the Earth rotation angle from `apsis-frames`.

```
use apsis_access::{AccessFinder, Pass};
use apsis_coverage::GroundPoint;
use apsis_geodesy::Geodetic;

// A station on the equator with a ten degree mask.
let station = GroundPoint::new(Geodetic::new(0.0, 0.0, 0.0), 10.0_f64.to_radians());
let finder = AccessFinder {
    station,
    sample_step_seconds: 60.0,
    crossing_tolerance_seconds: 0.001,
};
assert!(finder.is_valid());
```

Time-varying network topology.

A satellite network is not a single graph. The set of usable links
changes as the satellites move, so the network is a sequence of graphs
indexed by time, and this crate provides the data structures for that
sequence.

The model is deliberately simple. A [`Topology`] is a graph with a fixed
set of nodes, which are satellites or ground stations, and a set of edges
that may be present or absent. A [`TopologySeries`] is a sequence of
topologies at increasing times, together with the interval each one
applies to.

The series is a step function: a topology holds from its own time until
the time of the next entry. That is the standard discrete time model for
a time-varying graph, and it is what routing over the series expects.

Edges carry a cost as well as their presence, so that a routing algorithm
has something to minimise. The cost is a plain number, so a caller may
put a delay, a hop count, or anything else in it, as long as the routing
algorithm and the cost agree on the meaning.

```
use apsis_topology::{Topology, TopologySample, TopologySeries};

// Two satellites linked to each other.
let mut topology = Topology::new(2);
topology.add_edge(0, 1, 1.0).expect("the nodes exist");
assert!(topology.has_edge(0, 1));

let series = TopologySeries::new(vec![TopologySample::new(0.0, topology)])
    .expect("the series is not empty");
assert_eq!(series.len(), 1);
assert!(series.topology_at(0.0).expect("a topology exists").has_edge(0, 1));
```

Inter-satellite link geometry and visibility.

Two satellites can form a link when the straight line between them does
not pass through the Earth. This crate decides that condition, gives the
geometry a link budget needs, and reports the range and the angles at
which each satellite must point.

The blocking test is whether the line segment between the two positions
has any point inside the body. For a spherical body that is a
perpendicular distance test, which is exact and cheap, so it is the one
used here.

An optical link adds a second condition: the Sun must not be within the
field of view of either terminal, because the background would swamp the
signal. That is a separate test, offered as [`sun_blocks_link`], which
takes the direction to the Sun.

Lengths are in kilometers and angles in radians. The positions are in a
frame in which the Earth is a sphere of the given radius, which for link
geometry is the same frame the orbit layer produces.

```
use apsis_isl::{LinkGeometry, link_geometry, mutual_visibility};
use apsis_math::Vec3;

// Two satellites on the same side of the Earth see each other.
let first = Vec3::new(6_928.137, 0.0, 0.0);
let second = Vec3::new(6_868.137, 2_500.0, 0.0);
assert_eq!(mutual_visibility(first, second, 6_378.137), Some(true));

// Two satellites on opposite sides do not.
let opposite = Vec3::new(-6_928.137, 0.0, 0.0);
assert_eq!(mutual_visibility(first, opposite, 6_378.137), Some(false));
```

Mesh connectivity abstractions.

A satellite mesh is a set of terminals that reach each other through
links, some of which are satellite links and some of which are ground
links. This crate provides the abstractions that a mesh application needs
on top of a topology: which terminals can reach which others, how many
independent ways there are to do so, and which links are critical.

The three questions this crate answers:

- **Reachability**: the set of terminals reachable from a given one.
- **Redundancy**: how many independent paths join two terminals, which is
  the number of links that can fail independently. This is the
  edge-connectivity of the graph, by Menger's theorem.
- **Criticality**: the links whose removal would separate the mesh, which
  are the ones a network operator must protect.

The redundancy computation is exact for the small graphs a satellite mesh
presents. It uses the edge version of Menger's theorem, computing the
connectivity from a sequence of maximum flow problems, each of unit
capacity.

Source: Menger's theorem, and the reduction of edge connectivity to
maximum flow, as given in Cormen et al., *Introduction to Algorithms*,
3rd edition, MIT Press, 2009, chapter 26.

```
use apsis_mesh::Mesh;
use apsis_topology::Topology;

// Three terminals in a ring, so each pair has two independent paths.
let mut topology = Topology::new(3);
topology.add_bidirectional_edge(0, 1, 1.0).expect("the nodes exist");
topology.add_bidirectional_edge(1, 2, 1.0).expect("the nodes exist");
topology.add_bidirectional_edge(2, 0, 1.0).expect("the nodes exist");

let mesh = Mesh::new(topology);
assert!(mesh.is_connected());
assert_eq!(mesh.edge_connectivity(0, 1), Some(2));
```

Doppler shift computation.

A satellite and a ground station move relative to each other, so the
frequency that arrives at the receiver differs from the frequency that
left the transmitter. This crate computes that shift from the relative
velocity.

Two effects are present and they are separate. The classical shift is
proportional to the range rate divided by the speed of light, and it is
what a Doppler tracker compensates. The relativistic time dilation
between the two clocks is a second order correction that is far smaller
for orbital velocities, and it is offered separately so that a caller can
include it when the application needs it.

The sign convention used here is that a positive range rate, meaning the
two terminals are moving apart, produces a negative shift and therefore a
lower received frequency. This matches the everyday observation that a
receding source sounds lower.

```
use apsis_doppler::{DopplerShift, range_rate_from_velocity};
use apsis_math::Vec3;

// A satellite at the first axis moving away along it.
let range_rate = range_rate_from_velocity(
    Vec3::new(7_000_000.0, 0.0, 0.0),
    Vec3::new(1_000.0, 0.0, 0.0),
);
assert_eq!(range_rate, Some(1_000.0));

let shift = DopplerShift::from_range_rate(12.0e9, 1_000.0).expect("valid");
assert!(shift.shifted_frequency() < 12.0e9);
```

Atmospheric attenuation and refraction models.

A satellite signal passing through the atmosphere is weakened by two
mechanisms that are treated separately because they behave differently
with frequency, elevation, and weather.

Gaseous absorption comes from the absorption lines of oxygen and water
vapour. Oxygen has a dense group of lines between 50 and 70 GHz and an
isolated line at 118.75 GHz, and water vapour has a strong line at
22.235 GHz and a much stronger one at 183.31 GHz. The attenuation is the
line-by-line sum over those lines, which makes it negligible below about
10 GHz and severe in the absorption bands.

Rain attenuation is caused by scattering and absorption in
precipitation. It is negligible below about 4 GHz, grows as a power of
the rain rate, and depends strongly on elevation and polarisation.

Both are functions of the elevation angle through the same path
geometry: a signal at a low elevation crosses more atmosphere.

Sources:

- Gaseous specific attenuation and equivalent heights: ITU-R
  Recommendation P.676-12, *Attenuation by atmospheric gases*.
- Rain specific attenuation coefficients: ITU-R Recommendation P.838-3,
  *Specific attenuation model for rain for use in prediction methods*.
- Rain path and elevation dependence: ITU-R Recommendation P.618-13,
  *Propagation data and prediction methods required for the design of
  Earth-space telecommunication systems*.

```
use apsis_atmosphere::{Atmosphere, slant_path_attenuation, zenith_attenuation};

// At the reference atmosphere the 60 GHz oxygen complex is heavily
// attenuated while a Ku band link is barely affected.
let ku = zenith_attenuation(12.0, Atmosphere::REFERENCE).expect("valid");
let oxygen = zenith_attenuation(60.0, Atmosphere::REFERENCE).expect("valid");
assert!(ku < 0.1);
assert!(oxygen > 100.0);

// A lower elevation crosses more atmosphere.
let zenith = zenith_attenuation(12.0, Atmosphere::REFERENCE).expect("valid");
let low = slant_path_attenuation(12.0, 0.2, Atmosphere::REFERENCE).expect("valid");
assert!(low > zenith);
```

Interference and carrier-to-interference computation.

A wanted signal rarely arrives alone. This crate combines the wanted
carrier with one or more interfering carriers and with noise, and reports
the ratios that decide whether the wanted link is usable.

The figures computed here are:

- The carrier to interference ratio, over one interferer or several.
- The carrier to noise plus interference ratio, which is what a receiver
  actually sees, because it cannot separate the two.
- The equivalent noise temperature of an interferer, which lets an
  interference contribution be folded into the link budget as if it were
  noise.

Interference from a second satellite sharing the same frequency is
described by the angle between the two satellites as seen from the ground
station. The receive antenna has a sidelobe response in that direction,
and the ratio of the interferer's power to the wanted power follows from
the two gains and the two path losses. This crate takes the two gains as
inputs, so it composes with the pattern models in `apsis-antenna`.

Source: the definitions of carrier to interference and carrier to noise
plus interference, and the practice of expressing interference as an
equivalent noise temperature, are standard in satellite link design and
appear for example in the ITU-R Handbook on Satellite Communications.

```
use apsis_interference::{Interferer, carrier_to_interference, combine};
use apsis_rf::{Decibel, Power};

let wanted = Power::from_dbw(-130.0).expect("finite");
let interferer = Interferer {
    power: Power::from_dbw(-140.0).expect("finite"),
};

// One interferer ten decibels below the carrier leaves a C/I of 10 dB.
let ratio = carrier_to_interference(wanted, &[interferer]).expect("valid");
assert!((ratio.db() - 10.0).abs() < 1e-9);

// Combining with noise can only lower the result.
let combined = combine(ratio, Decibel::from_db(15.0)).expect("valid");
assert!(combined.db() < ratio.db());
```

Scheduling algorithms for satellite task planning.

A scheduler takes a list of tasks and a set of resources and produces a
schedule. This crate provides a greedy scheduler and the pieces needed to
build others.

Scheduling with resource constraints is intractable in general: deciding
whether every task fits is a bin packing problem, which is NP-hard. A
satellite planning problem is small, with hundreds of tasks rather than
millions, but an exact search is still impractical for a routine planning
cycle. The scheduler here is therefore a greedy heuristic.

The algorithm sorts the tasks and places each in the earliest time at
which all of its resources are free together. Tasks are considered in
decreasing order of weighted value, which is a good heuristic: the most
valuable work is placed first and the least valuable fills the remaining
gaps. Mandatory tasks are placed first regardless of their value, because
a task that must run is worth more than any task that may.

The result is not guaranteed to be optimal, and the crate reports what it
could not place rather than dropping it silently, so that an operator can
see the cost of the plan.

```
use apsis_resource::{Resource, ResourceId, ResourceSet, Window};
use apsis_scheduler::Planner;
use apsis_task::{Task, TaskId};

let mut station = Resource::new(ResourceId::new(0), 1.0);
station.set_windows(&[Window::new(0.0, 1_000.0)]).expect("usable");
let resources = ResourceSet::from_resources(vec![station]).expect("distinct");

let tasks = vec![Task::new(TaskId::new(0), 60.0)
    .try_require(ResourceId::new(0), 1.0)
    .expect("the resource is new")];

let planner = Planner::new();
let result = planner.schedule(&tasks, &resources);
assert_eq!(result.schedule.len(), 1);
assert!(result.is_complete());
```

Resource and time conflict detection.

A schedule can be wrong in several ways, and this crate finds them. It
reports conflicts rather than refusing them, so that a plan loaded from a
file can be checked in full and every problem listed at once instead of
one per attempt.

Five kinds of conflict are detected:

- **Capacity**: two placements need more of a resource at the same instant
  than the resource has.
- **Window**: a placement starts before its task may, or ends after it
  must.
- **Requirement**: a placement's task is not known, or its duration does
  not match the schedule.
- **Availability**: a placement falls outside the availability windows of
  a resource it needs, so the resource would not actually be there.
- **Precedence**: a task that must follow another starts before it ends.

The distinction between a capacity conflict and an availability conflict
matters in practice: the first says the plan is over committed, the second
says the plan asks for a resource at a time when it is switched off or out
of view, which is a different repair.

```
use apsis_resource::{Resource, ResourceId, ResourceSet, Window};
use apsis_schedule::Schedule;
use apsis_task::{Task, TaskId};
use apsis_conflict::{ConflictKind, detect};

let mut station = Resource::new(ResourceId::new(0), 1.0);
station.set_windows(&[Window::new(0.0, 1_000.0)]).expect("usable");
let resources = ResourceSet::from_resources(vec![station]).expect("distinct");

let task = Task::new(TaskId::new(0), 60.0)
    .try_require(ResourceId::new(0), 1.0)
    .expect("the resource is new");
let mut schedule = Schedule::new();
schedule.place(&task, 100.0).expect("the task fits");

let report = detect(&schedule, &[task], &resources);
assert!(report.is_clear());
assert_eq!(report.conflicts().len(), 0);
let _ = ConflictKind::Capacity;
```

Schedule representation and evaluation.

A schedule is the answer a scheduler produces: for each task, when it runs
and on which resources. This crate holds that answer and evaluates it.
It does not decide the placements, which is the job of `apsis-scheduler`;
keeping the two apart lets a schedule be read from a file, produced by a
scheduler, or written by hand, and evaluated the same way.

A placement records the task, its start time, and the resources it holds.
The resources are checked against the task's requirements when the
placement is made, so a schedule cannot describe a task running on a
resource it did not ask for.

The evaluation reports what an operator asks of a finished plan: how many
tasks were placed, how much of each resource is used at its busiest, and
how much of the available time was actually spent. Those figures are what
a planner compares between candidate schedules.

```
use apsis_resource::ResourceId;
use apsis_schedule::Schedule;
use apsis_task::{Task, TaskId};

let task = Task::new(TaskId::new(0), 60.0)
    .try_require(ResourceId::new(0), 1.0)
    .expect("the resource is not yet required");

let mut schedule = Schedule::new();
schedule.place(&task, 100.0).expect("the task fits");
assert_eq!(schedule.len(), 1);
assert!(schedule.is_placed(TaskId::new(0)));
```

Discrete-event simulation engine.

The engine advances a clock by jumping from one event to the next. It
holds the event queue, the current simulation time, and the statistics a
caller needs to judge a run. What an event means is the caller's: it
supplies a handler that receives the event and may schedule more events,
and the engine only sequences the calls.

The engine keeps the simulation deterministic. Events at the same instant
are delivered in the order they were scheduled, which the event queue
guarantees, so a run with the same inputs produces the same output. That
is what makes a result reproducible and a cross-check against another
implementation meaningful.

The engine stops for one of four reasons, reported as a
[`StopReason`]:
the queue empties, the simulation reaches its time limit, the caller asks
it to stop, or an event limit is reached. The last two exist so that a
model that schedules events forever cannot run without bound.

```
use apsis_sim_engine::Engine;

let mut engine = Engine::new();
engine.schedule(10.0, "hello");

let mut seen = Vec::new();
let outcome = engine.run(|engine, event| {
    seen.push(event.payload);
    // The handler schedules the next event, which the engine delivers
    // after this one. It schedules it only once, so the queue drains.
    if event.payload == "hello" {
        engine.schedule(20.0, "world");
    }
});

assert_eq!(seen, vec!["hello", "world"]);
assert_eq!(outcome.executed, 2);
assert_eq!(engine.now(), 20.0);
```

Simulation event types and event queue.

A discrete-event simulation advances by jumping from one event to the
next rather than by stepping through every instant. This crate provides
the queue that makes that possible: a priority queue ordered by time,
with a well defined order among events that fall at the same instant.

The tie-break matters more than it first appears. Two events scheduled
for the same instant must be delivered in a repeatable order, or the
simulation produces a different result on each run and a cross-check
against another implementation is impossible. Each event therefore
carries a sequence number assigned when it is scheduled, and events at
equal times are ordered by that number, so the order is the order in
which they were scheduled. That is the convention used by every
discrete-event simulator.

Times are in seconds from the start of the simulation, so the queue holds
no knowledge of calendars or time scales.

```
use apsis_event::EventQueue;

let mut queue = EventQueue::new();
queue.schedule(10.0, "second");
queue.schedule(5.0, "first");

// The earliest event comes out first.
let event = queue.pop().expect("the queue is not empty");
assert_eq!(event.time, 5.0);
assert_eq!(event.payload, "first");
```

items.doc:
Collects records as a simulation runs.

The recorder is the write side of a trace: a handler calls one of its
methods for each value it wants to keep, and asks for the trace when the
run finishes. The two are separate so that a handler can hold a recorder
while the trace is read elsewhere.

```
use apsis_output::Recorder;

let mut recorder = Recorder::new();
recorder.observe(0.0, "range", 1_000.0);
recorder.observe(10.0, "range", 950.0);

let summary = recorder.trace().summarize("range").expect("the series exists");
assert_eq!(summary.count, 2);
assert_eq!(summary.change, -50.0);
```

items.doc_zh:
在仿真运行过程中收集记录。

记录器是轨迹的写入侧：处理器为其想保留的每个值调用它的某个方法，
并在运行结束时索取轨迹。二者分开，使处理器可以持有记录器而轨迹
在别处被读取。

```
use apsis_output::Recorder;

let mut recorder = Recorder::new();
recorder.observe(0.0, "range", 1_000.0);
recorder.observe(10.0, "range", 950.0);

let summary = recorder.trace().summarize("range").expect("the series exists");
assert_eq!(summary.count, 2);
assert_eq!(summary.change, -50.0);
```

crates.summary:
Simulation facade that integrates the simulation layer.

This crate is the single entry point to the simulation layer. It ties the
four pieces together: a [`Scenario`] describes the initial conditions, the
ops layer plans it, the engine runs the resulting timeline, and a
[`Recorder`] collects what the run produced.

The sequence a caller follows is short:

1. [`Simulation::prepare`] checks a scenario and plans it, producing a
   schedule.
2. [`Simulation::start`] turns that schedule into a timeline of start and
   end events and hands back a [`Session`].
3. [`Session::run`] drives the engine, calling a handler at each event,
   and returns a [`RunReport`] holding the engine outcome and the trace.

The plan is a greedy heuristic, described in `apsis-scheduler`, so a run
may leave tasks unplaced. The facade reports that rather than hiding it:
[`Simulation::plan`] exposes the full planning result, and
[`Simulation::has_full_plan`] answers the common question directly.

The timeline is the schedule, not a physical model. A start event fires
when a task begins and an end event when it ends, which is what a caller
needs to record state over time; the handler supplies whatever physics or
bookkeeping the run requires.

```
use apsis_sim::{Activity, Scenario, Simulation};

let text = "\
scenario demo
resource 0 capacity 1
window 0 0 600
task 0 duration 60
require 0 0 1
";
let scenario = Scenario::parse(text).expect("the text is well formed");
let simulation = Simulation::prepare(scenario).expect("the scenario is usable");

let mut session = simulation.start();
let mut starts = 0_u32;
let report = session.run(|_engine, _recorder, event| {
    if matches!(event.payload, Activity::Start { .. }) {
        starts += 1;
    }
});

assert!(simulation.has_full_plan());
assert_eq!(starts, 1);
assert_eq!(report.outcome.executed, 2);
```
